course: dictionary
lesson: 9066
title: One word, two meanings: slime
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is being used. Slime can mean a thick, wet, slippery substance, like the trail a snail leaves behind. It can also mean a stretchy toy goo that children make from glue. Read the words around the word to decide.
explain: «Dry hands rub this slime off, so people who release fish wet their hands first.» The clue is fish. Slime here is the thick, slippery coating on a fish's skin, so it is the first meaning. Ask yourself: which meaning fits, and does it still make sense in the sentence?
explain: Phrases carry meaning too: «make slime», «a slime trail», «covered in slime». Slime is usually uncountable, so you say «some slime», not «a slime», when you mean the stuff itself.
fact: Slime is a noun, and it is usually uncountable, so you say «some slime».
fact: «Slime» means a thick, wet, slippery substance.
fact: «Slime» can also mean a stretchy toy goo that children make from glue.
fact: Snails and slugs use slime to move and stay moist.
fact: Some slime is neither quite a solid nor quite a liquid: «cornflour slime» can act like a solid one moment and a liquid the next.
exercise: «The slug left a silvery slime trail across the path.» What is slime in this sentence? | A stretchy toy made from glue | A type of cornflour cake | A kind of fish | *A thick, wet, slippery substance
exercise: «Make slime by mixing glue with a little water.» What does slime mean in this sentence? | A thick trail left by a snail | A wet fish | A dry powder | *A stretchy toy goo that children make from glue
exercise: «The gardener's boots were covered in slime after the rain.» Find and copy the phrase that shows slime is on something. | *covered in slime | boots were | after the rain | gardener's boots
test: «Some slime acts like a solid one moment and a liquid the next.» What kind of thing is slime here? | A kind of bird | *A substance that is not quite a solid and not quite a liquid | A dry sand | A hard metal
test: «Dry hands rub this slime off, so people who release fish wet their hands first.» Why do people wet their hands before they release fish? | *So they do not rub off the slime on the fish | So the fish can hear them coming | So they can make slime toys | So the fish turns green
exam: Pip spent the afternoon making slime from glue and water, then stretched it between her fingers. Which meaning of slime is used? | *A stretchy toy goo that children make from glue | A thick trail left by a snail | A kind of toad with a bright orange belly | A tall tree with thick bark
